Revision 22 was made during the design phase and lands round-6 decisions 10 and 14 together with
their ADR halves in one commit - the first requirements change made under the atomic process above.
It is NOT new scope: it amends one existing criterion, AC-24, in the two ways the design review
established were owed, and adds no rule, no constraint and no criterion.

DECISION 10 - AC-24's last clause counted rather than enumerated. It asked for a migration "for each
of the six FACTORY interfaces whose signature changed", and under this design no set of six answers
to that description: four of NFR-1's six change by their own signature (the two mapper factories and
the two transformer factories) and two through the shared base that ADR 0071 puts
CreatePipelineScope() on, while IAmAHandlerFactory and IAmALifetime break and are not on NFR-1's list
at all. The clause now names the eight interfaces outright - NFR-1's six together with
IAmAHandlerFactory and IAmALifetime. The two mapper registries stay outside it, exactly as before,
and are covered by extension of NFR-1(b) and NFR-1(c) on identical reasoning.

THE OWNER ACCEPTED THE BREAK RATHER THAN NARROWING IT, and that ruling is what replaces the ADRs'
arithmetic about whether the numeral had a referent: "As there is only one published implementer (MS
DI), we consider this an acceptable break for anyone who has their own DI implementation." The only
published implementations of these interfaces are the container-backed ones Brighter ships over
Microsoft's DI, so an application that hand-rolled its own takes a source and binary break on
netstandard2.0 and follows the migration the release-note entry states.

DECISION 14 - AC-24's four clauses reached four of the thirteen items in the single release_notes.md
entry, and its verifier ("one line per Then clause above") inherited the same four-clause reach, so
nine breaking changes could ship with nothing checking that they were written down. AC-24 gains a
general clause requiring that entry to contain one item per breaking change the work introduces, and
its verifier takes one line per item in that entry rather than one line for the clause. The other
three ACs sharing that verifier line - AC-25, AC-36 and AC-43 - keep one line per Then clause, and
so do AC-24's own first three clauses, which also govern the guidance page and would otherwise lose
their checks. Rejected: a new criterion enumerating one Then per break. The entry has grown 4 -> 7
-> 9 -> 10 -> 11 -> 12 -> 13 across six review rounds and the readability programme, so a hard-coded
count moves that drift into this document, where it costs more to correct.

No FR, NFR or constraint changes; no other AC changes. Counts unchanged at 27/10/52/123 - an AC
gaining a Then clause does not move the normative-bullet count, which counts FR and NFR clauses.

Why it needs no review of its own: beyond the process change above, it adds nothing. Both amendments
